diagnosis_text:
A complete end-to-end investigation of the Kath2 topology found no network fault whatsoever. All five CS department machines (cs_hod, cs_lab1 through cs_lab4) on the 192.168.2.0/24 subnet have correct IP addresses (192.168.2.2–.6) and the correct default gateway pointing at router0's eth1 (192.168.2.1). Every CS machine reaches pc1 (the server room host at 1.0.0.5) with 0% packet loss, and the reverse path from pc1 back to the CS lab is equally clean. The traceroute from cs_lab1 to pc1 confirms the correct three-hop forwarding path: CS machine → router0 → router1 → router2 → pc1.

The RIP routing plane is fully converged: all three routers hold complete, correctly-metriced routes to every subnet; router0 sees 1.0.0.0/8 at metric 3 via router1, and router2 holds 192.168.2.0/24 at metric 3 via router1. No iptables rules exist on any router. No RIP distribute-lists, passive-interface restrictions, or authentication mismatches are configured. Interface error counters are zero throughout. Both switch1 (CS-side) and switch4 (server-side) are bridging normally with both ports in forwarding state.

The helpdesk ticket describes a fault that does not exist in the current network state. This is a false-premise ticket: the CS department's reported inability to reach the server room is not supported by any measurable network anomaly. The corrective action is to re-examine the end-user application layer (e.g., whether a file-sharing service is actually running on pc1) rather than the network infrastructure.
=== END ===